\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{From real recurrences to the residue table}

\p{Hyperbolic reduction determines the matrix factor coming from matched
positive and negative axes. It leaves a one-sided algebra #{\Cl_{r,0}} or
#{\Cl_{0,r}}. The signature-switch recurrence of \ref{fcap-001X} provides a
second move, and \ref{fcap-0015} fixes the first real, complex, quaternionic,
and matrix entries. The one-sided recurrences now close this calculation.}

\p{The quaternion recurrence

##{\Cl_{p,q+2}\simeq
\Cl_{q,p}\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb H,}

is the actual real algebra equivalence
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordQuaternionRecurrenceEquiv}.
The eight-step result gives existence of the equivalence

##{\Cl_{p+8,q}\simeq
\Cl_{p,q}\otimes_{\mathbb R}M_{16}(\mathbb R).}

Its exact marker is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.nonempty_realCliffordEightPeriodicityEquiv}.}

\p{The residue-indexed mod-eight algebra classification is now formalized
as \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realClifford_classification}. The mixed
#{(1,1)} recurrence alone only removes matched axes; it cannot classify
the one-sided remainder. Lawson--Michelsohn give the classical table in
\citet{I.4, Theorem 4.3 and Tables I--II, pp. 27--29}{lawson2016spin}.}
